# Line-ending rules. Without these the deploy chain works on macOS and breaks
# on Windows, for a reason that is invisible until it happens.
#
# Git for Windows installs with core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites text files
# as CRLF on checkout. Every step of `npm run deploy` is a bash script, and
# bash treats a trailing \r as part of the command:
#
#   tool/build_web.sh: line 2: $'\r': command not found
#
# So a fresh clone on Windows can fail before the build has done anything,
# while the same commit deploys cleanly from a Mac. Pinning the scripts to LF
# is what makes the repo deployable from either machine.
#
# eol=lf, not text=auto: these files must be LF in the working tree on every
# platform, not merely normalised in the repository.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the other things bash and node read as scripts.
*.bash text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
